@page "/docs/registry"
<PageTitle>Registry - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Registry</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">distribution</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">static JSON</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl text-muted-foreground">A registry is how Blaizio distributes source. It is a set of JSON files on any static host: no server code, no database, no account. The official components come from one, and you can run your own to share components, enhanced variants of the built-ins, themes or fonts. Consumers install from it with the same <code>blaizio add</code> they already use.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Two layers of JSON</h2>
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                    <div class="text-sm font-semibold">The manifest</div>
                    <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">you edit</BzBadge>
                </div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground"><code>registry.json</code> is the source of truth. It names each item and points at its files on disk. Nothing in it is hosted.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                    <div class="text-sm font-semibold">The built output</div>
                    <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">you host</BzBadge>
                </div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">One <code>&lt;item&gt;.json</code> per item with every file's contents inlined, plus an <code>index.json</code> catalogue. <CmdChip Command="blaizio build" /> turns the manifest into it.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">The CLI resolves an item by fetching <code>&lt;registry-url&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.json</code>, so GitHub Pages or any static bucket is enough to publish one.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Who needs one</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A team with house components.</b> One registry, every app installs from it, and each app still owns the source it received.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A library author.</b> Ship components as source rather than as a package, so consumers can read and change them.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A theme author.</b> A theme item carries token values only, no files, and installs by patching the consumer's tokens file.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A monorepo.</b> A registry can be a local folder path, not just a URL, so a shared component tree works without publishing anything.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">This section</h2>
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
            @* Not `page` as the loop variable: `@page.X` is parsed as the @page directive. *@
            @foreach (var entry in DocsNav.Registry.Skip(1))
            {
                <a href="@entry.DocHref" class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 text-card-foreground no-underline transition-colors hover:border-primary/50">
                    <div class="text-sm font-semibold">@entry.Label</div>
                    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">@entry.Blurb</p>
                </a>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Versioning</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">A registry may stamp its items with a <code>version</code> (its own scheme - the CLI never orders versions, it only matches them). That enables pinning end to end:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><b class="text-foreground"><code>blaizio add button@1.2.0</code></b> installs exactly that version and records the pin in <code>blaizio.json</code>. <code>update</code> and <code>add --diff</code> re-request a pinned item at its pin; a later plain <code>add button</code> is the unpin.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Dependencies respect pins.</b> Installing an item whose dependency is pinned pulls the pinned version, never silently past it.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground"><code>nugetDependencies</code> pin with <code>Id@1.2.0</code>.</b> A bare id still floats to the latest.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Honest refusal.</b> A dynamic registry serves a pinned request from its history (the <code>?version=</code> query); a static one can only serve current - a mismatch fails the add cleanly instead of installing the wrong thing.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Agent access: <code>blaizio mcp</code> runs an MCP server so an assistant can search a registry and install from it - details on <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="docs/cli">the CLI page</a>.</p>
    </section>
</div>
